.TH MKCAT 1 "November 2026" "MK BBS" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mkcat \- show a CP437 text file (a BBS screen, a message, a DOS log) on the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkcat
[\fB\-u\fR | \fB\-c\fR] \fIFILE\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mkcat
shows a text file in the DOS character set (CP437), such as an ANSI art screen of a BBS, a message or a log, on a terminal of today.
Only the codes 128 to 255 are converted; control characters and escape sequences (the ANSI colours) go through unchanged.
A ^Z (the DOS end of file mark) ends the text.
The file name is used exactly as given.
.PP
Where the text is converted to UTF-8 the colour guard of the BBS is on: the text starts with light gray on black, and a colour reset
(ESC[0m) that sets no foreground or no background is followed by the missing DOS colour, so that the own default colours of a themed
terminal do not show through the art.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-u
UTF-8: the characters 128 to 255 are converted (box drawing, shading, accented letters).
.TP
.B \-c
CP437: the bytes as they are, for a terminal that speaks CP437 (a BBS terminal such as SyncTERM).
.PP
Without an option the output is guarded by where it goes: a terminal gets what its type wants
(\fBTERM\fR xterm, linux, screen and the like get UTF-8; ansi, syncterm and unknown types get CP437 as it is), and a file or a pipe gets UTF-8.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TERM
the terminal type, for the choice between UTF-8 and CP437
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
done
.TP
.B 1
the file could not be read
.TP
.B 3
a wrong command line
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
mkcat welcome.ans
mkcat \-u welcome.ans > welcome.txt
mkcat \-c welcome.ans | nc bbs.example.org 23
.fi
